00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:03.609
Maybe this is
some sort of manslaughter thing.

00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:06.411
But, certainly, he didn't go,
"Give me a loaded gun,

00:09:06.545 --> 00:09:08.247
I wanna shoot
the cinematographer."

00:09:08.447 --> 00:09:10.983
-I feel this is ridiculous.
-ARI: You want a legal view?

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.018
-BILL: Yes!
-Yeah, it's very overcharged.

00:09:13.785 --> 00:09:16.388
And it's hard
to imagine charging

00:09:16.688 --> 00:09:18.657
a normal random citizen,

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:21.560
a non-famous citizen twice
on this theory of the case.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:22.995
Now, legally,
the prosecutors say,

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:24.963
"Well, look,
as an involuntary manslaughter,

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.133 line:0%
we don't have
to prove deliberate intent,

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:28.800 line:0%
we're saying it was so reckless

00:09:28.867 --> 00:09:30.035 line:0%
and a life was lost
and it's tragic."

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:32.405 line:0%
And it is a terrible tragedy,
and obviously, some--

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:35.173 line:0%
a lot has to change on however
that set was ever run.

00:09:35.807 --> 00:09:37.877
But I don't think,
based on how it looks,

00:09:38.044 --> 00:09:38.977
and what we know,

00:09:39.277 --> 00:09:40.813
again, always
if more evidence came out,

00:09:41.046 --> 00:09:42.948
that a citizen,
a non-famous person

00:09:43.015 --> 00:09:44.216
would be double charged
like this.

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:46.052
-It seems very extreme.
-BILL: Okay. That's it.

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:47.919
Ran out of the time.
Thank you, CNN.

00:09:47.986 --> 00:09:49.388
Thank you, audience.
Thank you, panel.

00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:51.390
We'll see you next week.
Good to be back.

00:09:51.457 --> 00:09:52.892
(AUDIENCE CHEERING, APPLAUDING)

